      "context": "printed pp. 323-327 historical English body for easter-vigil / Blessing of the Baptismal Water.",
      "verified_on": "2026-08-19",
      "notes": "The longest of the fourteen and the one that took five leaves to read: n331 (p. 323), n332 (p. 324), n333 (p. 325), n334 (p. 326), n335 (p. 327). The 1862 at p. 201 prints the opening prayer `Omnipotens sempiterne Deus, adesto magnae pietatis tuae mysteriis, adesto sacramentis ...` under the heading Benedictio Fontis, and continues into the preface `Vere dignum et justum est ...`; its full opening conclusion includes `ejusdem` before `Spiritus sancti`, absent from the target and Mame 1922. The selected historical English excerpt spans the opening prayer, long blessing and three oil-and-chrism formulas. It omits the preface dialogue printed in Cummiskey at artifact PDF pp. 332–333 (printed pp. 323–324), as well as the initial greeting, and retains the source's abbreviated conclusion cues and historical cross positions. It is not a complete or exact performed-text rendering of the 1962 nn. 992–996; the printed `word by fire` remains unchanged. The book's ceremonial rubrics include italic directions and some bracketed text; this project selection omits them. The selected English is a normalized transcription, not diplomatic punctuation. The printed `the word by fire` is retained without correction. The closing `Thro’—who is to come` is the source's abbreviated conclusion cue followed by a dash; it is not expanded here. Alignment status in the normalized evidence artifact: candidate."
